<BaseLayout title="Teaching & reproducible activities" description="Teaching interests and a complete local activity about observations, model revision and planning. No student accounts, grades or tracking are collected."><section class="section"><div class="shell prose"><p class="eyebrow">TEACHING / LEARNING BY EXPLANATION</p><h1>Understand it.<br/><em>Then challenge it.</em></h1><p class="lead">Data mining, econometrics and data visualization, with an emphasis on assumptions, interpretation and reproducible work.</p><h2>Activity: when does the agent notice?</h2><p>Open the laboratory, run an unchanged episode, and write down the meaning of moves and ticks. Reset. Change the environment before the first step. Predict when the internal map will first change, then use One step to test your prediction.</p><h3>Questions</h3><ol><li>Why does the observer see the closed passage before the agent’s model changes?</li><li>Why can a correction consume a tick without increasing successful moves?</li><li>What changes when the model is frozen, while the sensor and planner are kept identical?</li><li>What does a verified replay prove—and what does it not prove?</li></ol><details class="card"><summary>Explanation and limits</summary><p>The model is revised only after local observation reveals a discrepancy. A revision tick changes the representation and route, not the position. A frozen model detects disagreement but keeps its prior and can be stopped by the common physical guard. Matching replay establishes consistency with one deterministic implementation; it does not establish authorship, generalization, learning performance or consciousness.</p></details><p>No accounts, submissions, grades or student data are stored by this activity.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="/projects/#agent-lab">Open the laboratory ↗</a><a class="button button-ghost" href="/research/notes/demo-protocol.html">Read the protocol ↗</a></div></div></section></BaseLayout>
